.TH TTY 4 "January 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "Device Drivers Manual"
.SH NAME
tty \- controlling terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /dev/tty
.SH DESCRIPTION
The special file
.B /dev/tty
is a synonym for the controlling terminal of the current process.
It can be used by programs that need to access the terminal explicitly,
regardless of standard input/output redirection.
.PP
On the kernel text console, keyboard input and
.B /dev/console
I/O resolve against the active virtual terminal first so the visible VT and
its line discipline stay aligned.
.PP
TTY state lives in ordinary kernel memory allocated via
.BR kmalloc (9) ,
not raw page-frame allocations.
.PP
The TTY core validates its live control object and driver callback table before
issuing indirect driver operations; corrupted or invalid callbacks fail with
.B EIO
instead of being executed.
.PP
Opening
.B /dev/tty
will fail with
.B ENXIO
if the process has no controlling terminal (e.g., daemons).
.SH EXAMPLE
Prompt user even when stdin is redirected:
.PP
.nf
    #include <fcntl.h>
    #include <unistd.h>

    int tty_fd = open("/dev/tty", O_RDWR);
    write(tty_fd, "Password: ", 10);
    read(tty_fd, buf, sizeof(buf));
.fi
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ENXIO
The process has no controlling terminal.
.TP
.B EIO
The active TTY or its backing driver callbacks were invalid.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/tty
Controlling terminal (character device, major 5, minor 0).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ttyname (3),
.BR isatty (3),
.BR intro (4)
